::PC_U02_L02_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
How many turning points can a degree 4 polynomial have at most?
{
=A. 3#Correct. A degree n polynomial has at most n-1 turning points.
~B. 4#This confuses degree with maximum turning points.
~C. 5#This is greater than the maximum possible number.
~D. 1#This may be too few for the maximum possible number.
}
::PC_U02_L02_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
What end behavior matches an odd degree polynomial with negative leading coefficient?
{
~A. left down, right up#This matches odd degree with positive leading coefficient.
=B. left up, right down#Correct. Odd degree with negative leading coefficient rises left and falls right.
~C. both ends up#This matches even degree with positive leading coefficient.
~D. both ends down#This matches even degree with negative leading coefficient.
}
::PC_U02_L02_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
What graph behavior is associated with odd multiplicity at a real zero?
{
~A. creates a vertical asymptote#Polynomial zeros do not create vertical asymptotes.
~B. touches and turns#Touching and turning is associated with even multiplicity.
=C. crosses the x-axis#Correct. Odd multiplicity usually means the graph crosses at the zero.
~D. removes the x-intercept#A real zero is an x-intercept.
}
::PC_U02_L02_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
Which key feature is determined by the leading term of a polynomial?
{
~A. all local extrema#Local extrema require more analysis than the leading term alone.
~B. exact y-intercept only#The y-intercept comes from evaluating at x=0.
~C. all zeros#Zeros require solving or factoring.
=D. end behavior#Correct. Degree and leading coefficient in the leading term determine end behavior.
}
::PC_U02_L02_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U02_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
A polynomial has zeros -2, 1, and 5. Which value is an x-intercept?
{
=A. 1#Correct. A real zero gives an x-intercept.
~B. 0#Zero is not listed as a zero.
~C. 3#Three is not listed as a zero.
~D. -5#The listed zero is 5, not -5.
}
